<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-insulin> a dmo:Recommendation ;
    rdfs:label "Management of Steroid Induced Hyperglycaemia with Insulin" ;
    dmo:gradingSystem "Not specified" ;
    dmo:nativeEvidenceGrade "Not specified" ;
    dmo:populationScope "People taking oral steroids with hyperglycaemia" ;
    dmo:recommendationId "management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-insulin" ;
    dmo:recommendationStrength "Unspecified" ;
    dmo:recommendationType "Treatment" ;
    dmo:statement "Morning administration of basal human insulin (Humulin I, Insuman Basal, Human Insulatard) may closely fit the glucose excursion induced by a single dose of oral steroid in the morning. We advocate the commencement of 10 units of basal human insulin" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Morning administration of basal human insulin (Humulin I, Insuman Basal, Human Insulatard) may closely fit the glucose excursion induced by a single dose of oral steroid in the morning. We advocate the commencement of 10 units of basal human insulin" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_08_hyperglycaemia_and_steroid_therapy_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-metformin> a dmo:Recommendation ;
    rdfs:label "Management of Steroid Induced Hyperglycaemia with Metformin" ;
    dmo:gradingSystem "Not specified" ;
    dmo:nativeEvidenceGrade "17" ;
    dmo:populationScope "People taking oral steroids with hyperglycaemia" ;
    dmo:recommendationId "management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-metformin" ;
    dmo:recommendationStrength "Unspecified" ;
    dmo:recommendationType "Treatment" ;
    dmo:statement "Metformin decreases gluconeogenesis and increases peripheral utilisation of glucose. Early indications are that metformin may reduce long term side effects from steroid use (17) but evidence of benefit in acute steroid induced hyperglycaemia is lacking." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Metformin decreases gluconeogenesis and increases peripheral utilisation of glucose. Early indications are that metformin may reduce long term side effects from steroid use (17) but evidence of benefit in acute steroid induced hyperglycaemia is lacking." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_08_hyperglycaemia_and_steroid_therapy_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-pioglitazone> a dmo:Recommendation ;
    rdfs:label "Management of Steroid Induced Hyperglycaemia with Pioglitazone" ;
    dmo:gradingSystem "Not specified" ;
    dmo:nativeEvidenceGrade "18; 19; 20" ;
    dmo:populationScope "People taking oral steroids with hyperglycaemia" ;
    dmo:recommendationId "management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-pioglitazone" ;
    dmo:recommendationStrength "Unspecified" ;
    dmo:recommendationType "Treatment" ;
    dmo:statement "This drug may seem an appropriate choice for the management of steroid induced hyperglycaemia. Pioglitazone may also take a number of weeks to achieve maximal effect. The evidence-base for the use of pioglitazone above other treatments described within this guideline however is weak (18; 19; 20)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "This drug may seem an appropriate choice for the management of steroid induced hyperglycaemia. Pioglitazone may also take a number of weeks to achieve maximal effect. The evidence-base for the use of pioglitazone above other treatments described within this guideline however is weak (18; 19; 20)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_08_hyperglycaemia_and_steroid_therapy_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-sulphonylureas-and-meglitinides> a dmo:Recommendation ;
    rdfs:label "Management of Steroid Induced Hyperglycaemia with Sulphonylureas and Meglitinides" ;
    dmo:gradingSystem "Not specified" ;
    dmo:nativeEvidenceGrade "8" ;
    dmo:populationScope "People taking once daily oral steroids with hyperglycaemia" ;
    dmo:recommendationId "management-of-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-with-sulphonylureas-and-meglitinides" ;
    dmo:recommendationStrength "Unspecified" ;
    dmo:recommendationType "Treatment" ;
    dmo:statement "By consensus, a short acting sulphonylurea, such as gliclazide, taken once daily may best manage the glucose excursion associated with a once daily oral steroid treatment. Whilst monitoring for hypoglycaemia the gliclazide may be titrated to a maximum of 240mg in the morning. An evening dose of gliclazide may also be initiated to achieve a maximum daily dose of 320mg (8)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "By consensus, a short acting sulphonylurea, such as gliclazide, taken once daily may best manage the glucose excursion associated with a once daily oral steroid treatment. Whilst monitoring for hypoglycaemia the gliclazide may be titrated to a maximum of 240mg in the morning. An evening dose of gliclazide may also be initiated to achieve a maximum daily dose of 320mg (8)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_08_hyperglycaemia_and_steroid_therapy_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/measure-baseline-hba1c-and-venous-plasma-glucose-when-starting-steroid-therapy-in-oncology-patients> a dmo:Recommendation ;
